# ktm (Ace 6) recovery-side touch enablement.
#
# 1) The HBP framework (oplus_hbp_core) registers the touch-00 IRQ at probe
#    time and then disables it; the panel-resume / hbp5-daemon handshake that
#    normally re-enables it never runs in recovery. Force-enable the kernel
#    IRQ gate once AERA module loading is complete.
# 2) The ODM hbp5 HAL daemon is refused by init in enforcing mode (the ODM
#    binary falls back to rootfs:s0, no domain transition; the seclabel
#    audit is fatal while the system is enforcing). Drop the system to
#    permissive so that audit is non-fatal and the ODM rc can start the
#    daemon; the daemon then completes its driver handshake.
#
# The TWRP recovery policy (system/sepolicy/private/twrp.te, compiled via
# the recovery_only macro) declares init/recovery/adbd permissive domains,
# so the /sys/fs/selinux/enforce write below succeeds under the TWRP tree.
# The sh service is a belt-and-braces fallback in case that attribute is
# missing from the live policy.
#
# A TWRP-style custom recovery running permissive is the community norm.

on early-init
    # Non-fatal if denied by policy: services start later either way.
    write /sys/fs/selinux/enforce 0
    start aera-selinux-perm

service aera-selinux-perm /system/bin/sh -c "echo 0 > /sys/fs/selinux/enforce"
    class main
    oneshot
    user root
    group root
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0

on property:post.decrypt.modules=true
    write /sys/devices/platform/soc/soc:hbp@0/irq_enable 0,1
